import type { InterviewType, Language } from "../types"

/*
 * Suggested spoken answers for the test/demo account ("Ali Test").
 *
 * Every answer is a SAMPLE written for a fictional early-career candidate in Malaysia (internship,
 * final year project, part time job). Companies, people and numbers are illustrative only. Answers
 * are written to be read aloud (about 40 to 60 seconds), contain no filler words and no dashes, and
 * behavioural / HR answers follow STAR with a number in the result so the STAR check passes.
 *
 * Lookup order in getSuggestedAnswer():
 *   1. Exact bank question (ANSWERS_EN / ANSWERS_MS), normalised.
 *   2. Exact follow-up question from generateProbingFollowUp (FOLLOW_UPS_EN / FOLLOW_UPS_MS).
 *   3. Resume / job-ad template question, with the skill, role or keyword pulled from the text.
 *   4. Follow-up-looking question, matched by wording (metrics, team, project, actions).
 *   5. Generic STAR answer per interview type that mentions the role.
 */

type AnswerMap = Record<string, string>

/* ======================================================================== */
/* English bank answers                                                     */
/* ======================================================================== */

const ANSWERS_EN: AnswerMap = {
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* HR: general                                                            */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "Tell me about yourself and what attracted you to this role.":
    "I'm a recent computing graduate from a public university in Malaysia. During my final year, I did a six month internship at a mid-sized logistics company, where my task was to help the operations team track late deliveries. I built a simple weekly report in Excel and later in Power BI, and as a result the team cut the time spent preparing it from about five hours to one. That experience showed me I enjoy turning messy problems into clear answers, and this role lets me do exactly that every day while learning from an experienced team.",
  "What are your key strengths, and what is one area you are actively working to improve?":
    "My key strength is that I'm organised and reliable with deadlines. In my final year project, our team of four had to deliver a working app in twelve weeks, and I was responsible for the timeline. I set up a shared task board and short weekly check-ins, and we finished one week early. The area I'm working on is public speaking. I used to rush through presentations, so I joined my faculty's presentation club and now practise every two weeks. As a result, my last presentation scored 85 percent, up from 70 the semester before.",
  "Why are you interested in joining our company over other opportunities?":
    "I'm interested in your company because you build products that everyday Malaysians use, and I want my work to have that direct impact. When I researched the team, I noticed you invest in training for fresh graduates, which matters to me because I want to grow properly in my first two years. During my internship, my goal was to become independent quickly, so I used real data every week and asked my mentor for feedback. As a result, I moved from basic SQL queries to writing reports used by 3 managers within four months. I want to keep that pace here, with a team that values learning and quality.",
  "What do you enjoy doing outside of work or study, and what has it taught you?":
    "Outside of study and work, I play futsal every week and I help organise a small community league in my neighbourhood. Last year, there was a problem with players not turning up, and I needed to fix it, so I set up a simple group schedule and a reminder message two days before each match. As a result, attendance went from around 60 percent to almost 90 percent over one season. It taught me that small, consistent systems make a big difference, and that keeping people informed is half the job. I bring that same habit into my work and projects.",
  "Describe a challenging work situation or tight deadline and how you handled the pressure.":
    "During my internship, our manager moved a client presentation forward by three days, and my task was to finish the sales analysis slides in time. First, I listed what was essential and what could wait, then I spoke with my supervisor to confirm the priorities. I blocked my calendar, focused on the three key charts, and asked a colleague to review my numbers early. As a result, we delivered the deck one day before the meeting, and the client approved the proposal. I learned that staying calm comes from having a clear plan, not from working longer hours.",
  "Where do you see your career going in the next three to five years?":
    "In the next three to five years, I see myself becoming a strong, independent contributor in this field and then starting to guide newer team members. In my first year, my goal is to master the core tools and understand the business deeply. During my internship, I set a similar target, so I started asking for small responsibilities early, and as a result I owned two weekly reports within three months. By year three, I aim to lead small projects from start to finish. By year five, I hope to mentor juniors, because helping classmates through tough subjects showed me how rewarding that can be.",
  "What type of workplace culture and management style helps you perform at your best?":
    "I perform best in a culture where people are open about problems early and where feedback is regular and specific. During my internship, my supervisor held a fifteen minute check-in with me every Monday. My goal was to deliver my main project early, and I used that time to share my progress and ask questions, and it helped me fix mistakes quickly instead of discovering them at the deadline. As a result, I finished my main project two weeks ahead of schedule. So I work best with a manager who sets clear goals, trusts me to plan my own work, and is available when I need guidance.",
  "How do you handle disagreements or different perspectives when working within a multidisciplinary team?":
    "In my final year project, I worked with two engineering students and a business student, and we disagreed on whether to add more features or polish the core app. My role was to keep the project on track. So I asked each person to explain their concern, then I suggested we look at our user feedback together. The data showed users mainly struggled with the login flow, so we agreed to fix that first. As a result, our usability score improved by 20 percent and we still added one extra feature. Shared data usually settles disagreements faster than opinions.",
  "What are your salary expectations for this role, and how did you arrive at that figure?":
    "Based on my research, I'm looking for a monthly salary in the range of 3,800 to 4,300 ringgit. I arrived at that figure by checking published salary guides and job listings for similar entry level roles in the Klang Valley, and by speaking with seniors from my university who work in this field. I also considered my internship experience and the skills I bring, such as SQL and dashboard reporting. That said, I'm flexible and I'll look at the full package, including training, medical benefits and growth opportunities, because learning in my first two years matters a lot to me.",
  "Tell me about a gap or a change of direction in your resume. What happened, and what did you learn from it?":
    "After my diploma, I took about eight months off before starting my degree. There was a family emergency, since my father fell ill, and I needed to help run our family's small food stall while he recovered. I handled the daily stock, the cash and two part time workers. I then started tracking our sales in a simple spreadsheet, and as a result we reduced wasted ingredients by about 15 percent. When he recovered, I returned to study with a much clearer reason for choosing this field. I learned responsibility, how to manage money carefully, and that data can help even a very small business.",
  "If you received two offers, ours and another with higher pay, how would you decide which one to accept?":
    "I'd compare the two offers on more than salary. I'd look at what I'll learn in the first two years, the quality of mentoring, the type of projects, and the long term growth path. When I chose my internship, I had to pick between two options, and I picked the one with a lower allowance because it gave me real project ownership. That choice paid off. As a result, I built a reporting dashboard used by 3 teams, which I now talk about in every interview. So if your role offers stronger learning and a clear path, I'd seriously consider it, even if the pay is slightly lower.",
  "What would your previous supervisor or lecturer say is the hardest thing about working with you?":
    "My internship supervisor would probably say that I sometimes take on too much myself instead of asking for help early. In my second month, I had to fix a broken report, and I tried to do it alone and lost almost two days. She pointed out that a five minute question would have saved me that time. So I set myself a rule: if I'm stuck for more than one hour, I ask. As a result, I finished my remaining tasks faster, and in my final review she rated my teamwork 4 out of 5, one of my strongest areas.",
  "Why should we hire you instead of another candidate with more experience?":
    "I may have less experience, but I learn fast, I'm reliable, and I've already delivered real results in a short time. During my six month internship, I was asked to automate a manual weekly report. So I learned Power BI on my own in three weeks and built the dashboard. As a result, the reporting time dropped from five hours to under one hour each week. I'm also eager to grow with your team for the long run, and I bring fresh ideas without old habits. You'd get someone who is hungry to learn and already proven to deliver.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* HR: role specific                                                      */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  // Associate Product Analyst
  "What product do you use every day, and what is one thing you would measure to know whether it is doing well?":
    "I use a food delivery app almost every day. If I were the product analyst for it, one thing I'd measure is the repeat order rate, meaning the share of users who order again within seven days. It shows whether people trust the app enough to come back. In my final year project, I tracked a similar repeat usage metric for a campus booking app, and it helped us notice a drop after an update. My task was to find the cause, so I checked loading times and suggested we fix a slow page, and as a result repeat usage rose from 35 to 48 percent in one month.",
  "Why do you want to start your career as a product analyst rather than in a pure data or business role?":
    "I enjoy data, but what excites me most is seeing analysis change a real product. In a pure data role, I'd worry about being far from the decision. In a pure business role, I'd miss the technical depth. During my internship, my task was to analyse why users dropped off in a signup form. I pulled the data with SQL, shared the findings with the product team, and they simplified two steps. As a result, completed signups rose by 18 percent in a month. That loop between data and product decisions is exactly where I want to start my career.",
  // Software Engineer
  "What got you into software engineering, and what kind of engineering work energises you most?":
    "I got into software engineering in secondary school when I built a simple website for my school's robotics club. Seeing other students use something I made was really motivating. At university, the work that energises me most is building features from start to finish and making them fast and reliable. In my final year project, I was responsible for the backend of a campus parking app. I built the API in Node.js and optimised the database queries, and as a result the average response time dropped from about two seconds to 300 milliseconds. Solving problems of that kind keeps me excited.",
  "How do you keep your technical skills up to date outside of your job or coursework?":
    "I keep my skills up to date with a simple weekly routine. I spend about three hours every weekend on one focused topic, such as testing or a new framework, and I always build something small with it. During my final year, I needed to learn TypeScript properly for a project, so I rebuilt one of my old JavaScript projects in it over six weeks. As a result, I found and fixed 12 hidden bugs that the type checker caught. I also read engineering blogs, join a local developer meetup once a month, and review open source pull requests to learn how experienced engineers write code.",
  // Data Analyst
  "What draws you to working with data, and what kind of questions do you enjoy answering?":
    "What draws me to data is that it turns arguments into answers. I enjoy questions where people have a strong opinion but no evidence yet. During my internship at a retail company, the store managers believed weekend promotions drove most sales, and my task was to check this. I pulled six months of sales data, cleaned it in Excel, and compared promotion weeks with normal weeks. Weekday lunch hours turned out to bring in 30 percent more revenue than expected. As a result, the team moved part of the promotion budget to weekdays and weekday sales grew 10 percent. Questions about what really drives behaviour are my favourite.",
  "How do you see the data analyst role changing in the next few years, and how are you preparing for it?":
    "I think the data analyst role will move away from manually building reports and more towards asking the right business questions, because automation and AI tools are handling a lot of the routine work. Analysts will need strong business sense, good communication and solid data foundations to check what the tools produce. My goal is to stay useful as tools change, so I'm strengthening my SQL and Python, and I recently completed a two month online statistics course. I also practise explaining findings in plain language. In my last project, I automated a weekly Excel report with Python, and as a result my team saved about four hours every week.",
  // Product Manager
  "Why product management, and what in your background prepares you for it?":
    "I'm drawn to product management because I enjoy sitting between users, technology and business, and helping everyone agree on what to build. In my final year project, I wasn't the strongest coder, but my role became organising our team of five. So I interviewed 15 students to understand their problem, wrote the feature list, and kept everyone aligned every week. As a result, we launched a study room booking app that 200 students used in its first month. My computing background helps me talk with engineers, and leading that project prepared me for the coordination side.",
  "Product managers often have responsibility without authority. How do you feel about that, and how would you work with it?":
    "I'm comfortable with it, because I've already worked that way. In student projects and at my internship, I rarely had authority over anyone, yet I still needed people to deliver. What works for me is building trust and making the reason behind each decision clear. During my internship, I needed to get the design and engineering interns to adjust a feature within one week. So I explained the user data behind the request, asked what they needed from me, and removed small blockers for them. As a result, we shipped on time and usage of the feature grew 25 percent.",
  // UX Designer
  "What got you interested in UX design, and which product do you think has a great user experience?":
    "I got interested in UX when I watched my grandmother struggle with a banking app, even though she's very capable. I realised design decides who can use technology and who gets left out. One product I think has a great user experience is the note taking app I use daily, because it's simple on day one but gets more powerful as you learn it. In my final year project, my goal was to follow the same idea. I tested our app with 8 users and simplified the home screen, and as a result task completion improved from 60 to 90 percent.",
  "How do you react when users or stakeholders strongly dislike a design you are proud of?":
    "I stay curious rather than defensive, because a design only matters if it works for the people using it. In my final year project, I designed a dashboard I was really proud of, but in testing, 4 out of 6 users found the navigation confusing. My goal was to fix it before the final presentation, so I asked them to show me where they got stuck, took notes, and then simplified the menu from seven items to four. As a result, all 6 users finished the main task in the next round of testing. That taught me to love the problem, not my first solution.",
  // Customer Support
  "Why do you want to work in customer support, and what does good service mean to you?":
    "I want to work in customer support because I enjoy solving problems for people and seeing their mood change from stressed to relieved. For me, good service means listening properly, being honest about what I can do, and following through until the issue is closed. At my part time job in a phone shop, a customer came back angry about a faulty charger. My task was to make it right, so I listened, replaced it on the spot, and showed him how to check the cable. As a result, he returned a week later with two friends who bought from us. Moments such as that show why good service matters.",
  "Support work can involve shifts, weekends and public holidays. How do you manage your energy and stay motivated?":
    "I'm used to irregular hours, because during my studies I worked weekend and evening shifts at a café for almost two years. I needed to manage my energy well, so I kept a fixed sleep routine even on shift weeks, prepared my meals in advance, and planned my rest days properly. I also remind myself why the work matters. On busy public holiday shifts, our team served about 300 customers a day, and I focused on keeping each one happy. As a result, I received the staff of the month award twice. For me, motivation comes from routine and from seeing the impact on customers.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Behavioural: general                                                   */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "Tell me about a time you worked in a team to complete a project. What was your contribution?":
    "In my final year, our team of four built a mobile app for booking campus sports courts. My role was to handle the database and the booking logic. I designed the database tables, wrote the API for creating and cancelling bookings, and set up a check to stop double bookings. I also ran short weekly syncs so we caught problems early. As a result, we delivered two weeks ahead of the deadline, around 150 students used the app in the first month, and our project received an A grade from the panel.",
  "Describe a time you learned a new skill or tool quickly to get something done.":
    "During my internship, my supervisor asked me to build a sales dashboard in Power BI, but I'd never used it before and the deadline was two weeks away. I needed to learn fast without slowing the team down. So I spent the first three evenings on a free online course, then I rebuilt one of our existing Excel reports in Power BI as practice. When I got stuck, I searched the community forums before asking my supervisor. As a result, I delivered the dashboard in ten days, and it replaced a manual report that took four hours every week.",
  "Give an example of a time you helped a teammate or classmate who was struggling.":
    "In my second year, a classmate in my programming group was struggling with object oriented concepts and was close to failing the subject. Our group project depended on everyone contributing code, so I needed to help him without doing the work for him. I suggested we meet twice a week for one hour, and I explained the concepts using small examples from our own project. I then let him write the code while I reviewed it. As a result, he completed his part of the project on time and scored 72 percent in the final exam.",
  "Describe a challenging project you contributed to. What was your specific role and the measurable outcome?":
    "During my internship at a logistics company, the operations team was losing track of late deliveries because the data sat in five separate spreadsheets. My task was to combine them into a single weekly report. So I mapped the columns, wrote SQL queries to merge the data, and built a dashboard showing late deliveries by region and driver. I also trained two team members to use it. As a result, the team spotted the worst routes quickly, and late deliveries dropped by 12 percent within two months.",
  "Tell me about a time you had a conflict with a teammate or stakeholder. How did you resolve it?":
    "In a group assignment, one teammate kept submitting his part late, which delayed everyone. I was the group leader, so I had to address it without damaging our working relationship. I spoke to him privately and asked what was happening instead of blaming him. It turned out he was working part time at night. We agreed to swap tasks so he had the parts with more flexible deadlines, and I set up a shared tracker. As a result, he delivered every remaining task on time, and our group scored 82 percent for the project.",
  "Give an example of an ambitious goal you set for yourself and the steps you took to achieve it.":
    "In my final year, I set myself a goal to get an internship in a data role, even though my course was general IT and I had no data experience. I needed to build a portfolio to prove my skills. I studied SQL and Power BI for one hour every night for three months, and I built three small projects using public Malaysian datasets, such as rainfall and population data. I then shared them on my professional profile and asked seniors for feedback. As a result, I got four interview calls and accepted an internship offer within six weeks.",
  "Describe a time a project did not go according to plan. What lessons did you learn from the setback?":
    "In my final year project, we planned to collect data through a survey, but after two weeks we had only 20 responses out of the 150 we needed. My task was to recover the data collection without missing our deadline. I spoke to our supervisor, then shortened the survey from 25 questions to 10 and shared it through class representatives instead of just posting it online. As a result, we reached 170 responses in one week. The lesson I learned was to test a plan early with a small group and to have a backup channel ready from the start.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to persuade team members or clients to adopt your recommended approach.":
    "During my internship, the team was sending customer updates manually by email, which took about six hours a week. I believed a simple automated template would save time, but my colleagues worried it would feel less personal. I needed to convince them without forcing the change. I built a small prototype, tested it with 30 customers alongside the manual emails, and showed the team the results. Response rates were almost the same, and the automated version took one hour a week. As a result, the team adopted it and we saved about five hours every week.",
  "Describe a decision you made with incomplete information. How did it turn out, and what would you change?":
    "During my internship, I had to choose between two data sources for a monthly cost report, but one was missing three weeks of records and my supervisor was on leave. The report was due in two days. So I compared both sources on the weeks they overlapped, found they matched within 2 percent, and used the complete one while clearly noting the gap. As a result, the report went out on time, and when the missing data arrived, the totals changed by less than 1 percent. Next time, I'd also check with another senior colleague earlier instead of deciding alone.",
  "Tell me about a mistake you made that affected other people. How did you handle it, and what did you change afterwards?":
    "During my internship, I sent out a weekly sales report with a filter error, so one region's numbers were doubled. Two managers had already used it in a meeting before I noticed. I had to fix it fast, so I immediately told my supervisor, sent a corrected report with a short note explaining what went wrong, and apologised to both managers. Then I added a checking step where I compare totals against the source system before sending. As a result, I haven't had a reporting error since, across more than 20 weekly reports. I learned that owning a mistake quickly builds more trust than hiding it.",
  "Describe a situation where you had to push back on a request from someone more senior than you.":
    "During my internship, a senior manager asked me to add three extra charts to a dashboard the day before it was due for the whole department. I knew rushing them would risk wrong numbers. My task was to deliver something reliable without simply saying no. So I explained the risk politely, showed him the current timeline, and proposed delivering the main dashboard on time and the extra charts two days later. He agreed. As a result, the dashboard launched on schedule with zero errors, and the additional charts followed within 48 hours.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Behavioural: Associate Product Analyst                                 */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "Tell me about a time you used data to answer a question or make a decision, even a small one.":
    "During my final year, our student club wanted to know the best day to hold workshops because attendance was dropping. My task was to find out with real data instead of guessing. I collected registration and attendance records from the past 12 events and organised them in a spreadsheet by day and time. The pattern was clear: Wednesday evening sessions had almost double the attendance of Friday ones. I then suggested moving all workshops to Wednesdays. As a result, average attendance rose from 25 to 42 people over the next semester.",
  "Describe a time you had to explain a number or a chart to someone who found it confusing.":
    "During my internship, a sales manager was confused by a chart showing that conversion rate fell even though total orders went up. He thought the chart was wrong. My task was to explain it clearly. So I sat with him and used a simple example: more visitors came from a new campaign, but fewer of them bought. Then I split the chart by traffic source so he could see it himself. As a result, he understood within ten minutes, and the team adjusted the campaign targeting, which improved conversion by 8 percent the following month.",
  "Tell me about a time you were curious about why something happened and dug into it on your own.":
    "During my internship, I noticed that app support tickets spiked every Monday, but nobody had looked into it. I was curious, and my goal was to find the cause on my own. So I pulled three months of ticket data and grouped the tickets by topic and day. I found that most Monday tickets were about password resets, because a weekly security setting logged users out on Sunday night. I shared this with the product team. As a result, they changed the setting, and Monday tickets dropped by about 35 percent within a month.",
  "Tell me about a project where you used data or metrics to improve a product or an operational process.":
    "During my internship at an e-commerce startup, customers often abandoned their cart at the shipping step. My task was to find out why and suggest a fix. I used SQL to pull funnel data and found that 40 percent of the users who left did so right when the shipping fee first appeared. I then compared it with user feedback, which confirmed the fee came as a surprise. I proposed showing the estimated fee earlier on the product page. As a result, checkout completion improved by 11 percent in the next month.",
  "Describe a time you received critical feedback on an analysis. How did you respond?":
    "During my internship, I presented an analysis on why sales dropped, and my supervisor pointed out that I hadn't considered seasonality. At first I felt embarrassed, but I knew she was right. My task was to fix the analysis before it reached management. I thanked her, asked for one example of how she would handle it, and then compared the same months from the previous two years. The real drop was only 4 percent, not the 15 percent I had reported. As a result, management avoided an unnecessary 10 percent price cut, and I now always check seasonality first.",
  "Walk me through a situation where you had to present complex findings to non-technical business leaders.":
    "During my internship, I had to present a customer churn analysis to the operations director and two department heads, none of whom had a technical background. My goal was to make the findings clear in ten minutes. So I removed the statistical details, opened with one key message, and used a single chart showing that customers who waited more than three days for delivery were twice as likely to leave. I ended with two clear recommendations. As a result, the director approved a faster delivery option for priority areas, which reduced churn by 6 percent over the next quarter.",
  "How have you prioritised product features or bug fixes when stakeholders had competing requirements?":
    "During my internship, the sales team wanted a new discount feature, while customer support wanted us to fix a bug that caused failed payments. My task was to help the product lead decide. So I estimated the impact of each request. The payment bug affected about 3 percent of transactions, roughly 400 orders a week, while the discount feature was a nice extra. I presented both numbers in a simple table and recommended fixing the bug first. The team agreed, and as a result, failed payments dropped to under 0.5 percent and the discount feature followed two weeks later.",
  "Tell me about a time you identified an unmet user need and turned it into actionable user stories.":
    "During my internship, I was reviewing app feedback and noticed many users asking how to split a bill with friends. My task was to check whether this was a real need. I counted the requests and found it was mentioned in about 120 reviews over three months. I then interviewed five users to understand how they split bills today. I wrote three user stories, for example: as a user, I want to split a payment with friends so that I don't need to calculate it myself. As a result, the feature launched with 15 percent adoption in its first month.",
  "Describe a time your analysis led to a recommendation that turned out to be wrong. What did you do next?":
    "During my internship, my analysis suggested that sending a promotional notification at 9 pm would increase orders, so the team tried it. After two weeks, orders hardly changed and app uninstalls went up slightly. My task was to understand what went wrong. So I looked again and realised my original data only covered university students, not our full user base. I shared this openly with the team, then designed a proper test across all user groups. As a result, we found that 6 pm worked better, lifting orders by 7 percent. I learned to always check whether my sample matches the real audience.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Behavioural: Software Engineer                                         */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "Tell me about a coding project, at work, at university or on your own, that you enjoyed building. What was your part?":
    "In my final year, I built a campus parking app with three classmates, and my role was the backend. I designed the database, built the REST API in Node.js, and wrote the logic that updated parking availability every 30 seconds. I also wrote unit tests for the booking rules. The hardest part was handling two people booking the same spot at once, which I solved using a database transaction. As a result, we demoed it to the faculty with 50 test users, and it won second place in our faculty's project showcase.",
  "Describe a time you had to learn a new language or framework quickly for a project.":
    "During my internship, the team used React, but I'd only learned plain JavaScript at university. I was given a small feature to build within two weeks, so I needed to learn fast. I spent the first two evenings on the official tutorial, then I read through our existing codebase to understand the team's patterns. I started by fixing two small bugs so I could learn safely, and I asked my mentor to review my code early. As a result, I delivered the feature, a filter panel for the orders page, in nine days, and it passed code review with only minor comments.",
  "Tell me about a time you asked for help on a technical problem. How did you decide when to ask?":
    "During my internship, I was stuck on a bug where our API returned the wrong time for orders, and I needed to fix it before an afternoon release. I tried for about an hour, checking the code and adding logs, but couldn't find the cause. My rule is to spend one focused hour first, write down what I've tried, and then ask. So I messaged a senior engineer with a short summary of my findings. She spotted within ten minutes that the server used UTC while the database used Malaysia time. As a result, I fixed it the same day and saved what could have been two days of guessing.",
  "Describe a challenging bug or performance bottleneck you investigated and resolved.":
    "During my internship, the order history page on our web app took about eight seconds to load for active customers. My task was to find the cause. I used the browser developer tools and the database query log, and found the page was running one query per order, more than 200 queries for some users. I rewrote it as a single query with a join and added an index on the customer ID column. As a result, the load time dropped to under one second, and support complaints about the page dropped to almost zero.",
  "Walk me through a situation where you disagreed with a technical design decision. How was it resolved?":
    "In my final year project, our team lead wanted to store all app data in one large JSON file to save time. I was worried it would break when several users saved at once. I needed to raise this without slowing the team down. I built a small test that simulated 20 users saving together, and it corrupted the file twice. I showed the results and suggested using a simple SQLite database instead, which would only take one extra day. The team agreed. As a result, we had zero data loss during our demo with 40 users.",
  "Describe a project where you introduced or improved automated testing or a CI/CD pipeline.":
    "During my internship, our team often found bugs only after deploying, because tests were run manually and sometimes skipped. My task was to improve this. I set up a CI pipeline on our Git repository that ran unit tests and a linter on every pull request. I also wrote tests for the 10 most used API endpoints. At first, some colleagues felt it slowed them down, so I made the pipeline finish in under four minutes. As a result, bugs reaching production dropped by about 40 percent over the next two months.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to balance shipping code quickly against keeping the architecture clean.":
    "During my internship, we had one week to release a promotion banner for a sales campaign. The quickest way was to hard code it into three pages, but that would be messy to maintain. My task was to deliver on time without creating a long term problem. I proposed building one small reusable banner component first, which took one extra day, and leaving advanced settings for later. I wrote a short note listing what we skipped. As a result, we launched on time, and the next campaign reused the component in two hours instead of two days.",
  "Tell me about a time you mentored a junior engineer or pushed for better code review practices.":
    "During my internship, two newer interns joined our team, and I noticed their pull requests often sat for days without review. I was asked to help them settle in. So I paired with each of them for one hour a week, and I suggested a simple review checklist for the team covering naming, tests and error handling. I also offered to do first reviews within one working day. As a result, the average review time dropped from three days to one, and both interns had their first features merged within their second week.",
  "Describe a production incident you were involved in. What was your role, and what changed afterwards?":
    "During my internship, our payment confirmation emails stopped sending one evening after a deployment. I was on the support rotation with a senior engineer, and my role was to find the cause while she handled communication. I checked the logs and found that a new environment variable for the email service was missing in production. We added it and resent the 180 pending emails within an hour. Afterwards, I wrote the incident report and added a startup check that fails the deployment if required variables are missing. As a result, we've had zero similar incidents in the 6 months since.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Behavioural: Data Analyst                                              */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "Tell me about a report or dashboard you built. Who used it, and what did they use it for?":
    "During my internship at a retail company, I built a weekly sales dashboard in Power BI for the regional managers. Before that, they received a large Excel file every Monday and had to find their own numbers. My task was to make it faster to read. I connected the dashboard to the sales database, added filters by store and product category, and highlighted stores below target. The managers used it in their Monday meeting to decide which stores needed support. As a result, report preparation time dropped from four hours to 30 minutes each week.",
  "Describe a time you found an error in a spreadsheet or report. What did you do about it?":
    "During my internship, I noticed that a monthly revenue report showed a 25 percent jump for one branch, which seemed too high. My task was to check it before it went to management. So I traced the numbers back and found that some refunds had been entered as sales because of a wrong formula reference. I corrected the formula, rechecked the totals against the finance system, and informed the report owner with a short explanation. As a result, the true growth was shown as 4 percent, and we added a total check to the template so it wouldn't happen again.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to learn a new data tool, such as Power BI, Tableau or Python, for a task.":
    "During my internship, my supervisor asked me to clean and combine 12 monthly sales files, which would take days in Excel. I'd only used Python a little at university, so I needed to learn pandas quickly. I spent two evenings on a short tutorial, then I wrote a small script step by step, testing each part on one file first. When I got stuck on merging, I checked the documentation and asked a colleague once. As a result, the script combined all the files in under a minute, and the team reused it every month, saving around six hours of manual work.",
  "Walk me through an end-to-end analytics project, from data extraction and cleaning to dashboard delivery.":
    "During my internship, the marketing team wanted to know which customer groups responded best to our promotions. My task was to deliver a dashboard they could use. First, I extracted 12 months of order data with SQL. Next, I cleaned it in Python, removing duplicates and fixing missing postcodes. Then I grouped customers by spending and frequency, and built a Power BI dashboard showing response rates by segment. I walked the team through it and wrote a one page guide. As a result, they targeted the top two segments and improved promotion response by 15 percent.",
  "Describe a situation where the data was messy, incomplete or contradictory. How did you handle it?":
    "During my final year project, I analysed public transport usage data, but the files had missing dates, duplicate rows and station names spelled in different ways. My task was to make it reliable before analysis. I first profiled the data to see how big each problem was. Then I removed exact duplicates, standardised station names with a mapping table, and flagged missing dates instead of deleting them. I documented every cleaning step. As a result, I kept 96 percent of the original records, and my supervisor said it was the most reliable dataset in our cohort.",
  "Tell me about a time someone asked you for data without a clear question. How did you find out what they really needed?":
    "During my internship, a manager asked me for all customer data from last year. I knew sending a huge file wouldn't help, so my task was to find the real question. I asked him what decision he was trying to make. It turned out he wanted to know which customers had stopped buying, to plan a win back campaign. I then pulled only inactive customers with their last purchase date and total spend. As a result, he got a focused list of 850 customers in one day instead of 50,000 rows, and the campaign won back about 9 percent of them.",
  "Tell me about a time your insights influenced an important business decision.":
    "During my internship at a food distribution company, management was planning to open a second warehouse in the north. My task was to analyse delivery data to support the decision. So I mapped six months of deliveries by area and delivery time, and found that 60 percent of late deliveries came from the southern region, not the north. I presented this with a clear map and a cost comparison. As a result, management chose to open the new warehouse in the south first, and late deliveries fell by 20 percent in the following quarter.",
  "Describe a time your analysis showed something stakeholders did not want to hear. How did you present it?":
    "During my internship, the marketing team was proud of a social media campaign, but my analysis showed it brought many visitors and very few buyers. I knew they wouldn't want to hear that, so my task was to present it fairly. I started with what worked, the strong reach, then showed that conversion was only 0.4 percent compared with 2 percent from email. I suggested a small test to improve the landing page rather than stopping the campaign. As a result, the team accepted the findings, and the follow up test raised the campaign's conversion rate from 0.4 to 0.8 percent.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to deliver an analysis under a very tight deadline. What did you cut, and why?":
    "During my internship, the finance manager needed a cost breakdown for a board meeting with only one day's notice. My task was to deliver something accurate in time. I agreed with her that the board only needed the top level view, so I cut the detailed product breakdown and the interactive dashboard I'd planned. I focused on five key cost categories, checked them against the accounting totals, and noted what was left for later. As a result, she had the numbers four hours before the meeting, and I delivered the full breakdown the following week.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Behavioural: Product Manager                                           */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "Tell me about a product you love and one feature you would change. Why?":
    "I love using a public transport journey planner app because it helps me plan trips around the city quickly. One feature I'd change is the arrival alert. Right now, it only tells me when to leave, but not when I'm close to my stop. In my final year project, we tested a similar idea in our campus shuttle app, and my task was to gather feedback. I interviewed 12 students and added a stop alert based on location. As a result, missed stop complaints dropped by half, so I believe the same change would help many commuters.",
  "Describe a time you organised a group of people to deliver something on time.":
    "During my second year, I led a team of eight volunteers to organise a career fair for our faculty in just four weeks. My role was to coordinate everyone and keep the plan on track. I split the work into three small groups, set clear weekly deadlines, and used a shared checklist so everyone could see progress. When the sponsorship group fell behind, I helped them contact companies directly. As a result, we delivered the event on time, with 15 employers and over 400 students attending, which was double the previous year.",
  "Tell me about a time you talked to users or customers to understand a problem.":
    "In my final year project, we wanted to build an app to help students find study rooms, but we weren't sure what the real problem was. My task was to understand it before we wrote any code. So I interviewed 15 students at the library and asked them to walk me through how they found a room last time. I learned the real issue wasn't finding rooms, it was not knowing if they were already taken. So we focused on live availability. As a result, 200 students used the app in its first month.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to say no to a feature request from an important stakeholder.":
    "During my internship, the head of sales asked us to add a custom report feature for one large client, two weeks before a major release. My task was to help the product lead decide. I estimated it would take about 10 days and delay a feature that 5,000 users were waiting for. So I met him, explained the trade off clearly, and proposed a simple export to Excel as a temporary solution. He agreed. As a result, the release went out on time, and the client was satisfied with the export, which took only one day to build.",
  "Describe a product or feature you launched. How did you decide it was ready, and how did you measure success?":
    "During my internship, I helped launch a saved address feature for a delivery app. My task was to check readiness and define success. Before launch, we ran the feature with 50 internal users for one week and fixed three bugs. I then set a clear rule that it was ready when no critical bugs remained and the checkout flow stayed stable. For success, I tracked how many users saved an address and how long checkout took. As a result, 30 percent of users saved an address in the first month, and average checkout time fell by 20 seconds.",
  "Describe a time you had to change a plan halfway because new information came in.":
    "In my final year project, we planned a food ordering app for campus cafés, but halfway through, the university announced its own ordering system. My task as the coordinator was to save the project. I called a team meeting, we reviewed what we'd built, and we found our menu and payment features could support campus events instead. I then rewrote the plan and reordered our priorities with our supervisor. As a result, we changed direction within one week, delivered on time, and three student clubs used the app for their event ticket sales.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to trade off scope, quality and deadline. What did you choose, and why?":
    "During my internship, we had three weeks to launch a referral feature before a festive campaign, but the full scope needed five weeks. My task was to help decide what to give up. So I listed every part of the feature and asked which ones users really needed at launch. I suggested we keep quality high and the deadline fixed, but cut the scope, dropping the leaderboard and custom rewards. As a result, we launched on time with no major bugs, and the referral feature brought in 1,200 new users during the campaign.",
  "Describe a situation where engineering, design and business teams disagreed on direction. How did you align them?":
    "During my internship, the design team wanted a full redesign of the signup flow, engineering wanted a smaller fix, and the business team wanted it done before a campaign in three weeks. My task was to help align them. I organised one meeting where each team shared their main concern, and then I showed data that 70 percent of users who quit did so at a single step. I proposed fixing that step first and planning the full redesign later. Everyone agreed. As a result, we launched in two weeks, and signups rose by 18 percent.",
  "Tell me about a product decision you made that turned out badly. What did you learn?":
    "In my final year project, I decided to require users to create an account before seeing any content in our study app, because I wanted more registered users. It turned out badly. In the first week, 60 percent of visitors left at the signup screen. My task was to fix it quickly. I spoke with five users, who said they wanted to try the app first. I then changed the flow so users could browse freely and sign up only when saving notes. As a result, signups grew by 35 percent. I learned to test assumptions before turning them into rules.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Behavioural: UX Designer                                               */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "Walk me through a design project in your portfolio. What problem were you solving?":
    "One project in my portfolio is a redesign of a clinic appointment booking app for my final year. The problem was that older patients struggled to book, and many called the clinic instead. My task was to make booking simple enough to finish without help. I interviewed 8 patients, mapped the booking steps, and found the date picker and small text caused most confusion. I designed a larger, step by step flow and tested two prototypes. As a result, booking completion in testing rose from 55 to 90 percent, and average time dropped by almost half.",
  "Tell me about a time you received feedback on your design. What did you change?":
    "During my internship, I shared a design for a new settings page, and a senior designer said the layout looked clean but users wouldn't find the notification options. My task was to fix it before development. So I asked her for specific examples, then ran a quick test with 5 colleagues. Three of them couldn't find the notifications section. I moved it to the top, renamed the labels in plain language, and grouped related settings. As a result, all 5 testers found it within ten seconds in the next round, and the design was approved for development.",
  "Tell me about a time you designed something for people very different from yourself.":
    "In my final year project, I designed a medication reminder app for elderly users, most of them over 65, which is very different from me. My task was to understand their needs properly. I visited a community centre and spent time with 10 elderly users, watching how they used their phones. I noticed they struggled with small buttons and swipe gestures. So I designed large buttons, clear text, and simple single tap actions. As a result, task success in testing improved from 50 to 85 percent, and several users said it was the first app they could use alone.",
  "Describe a time user research changed your original design direction.":
    "During my internship, we planned to add a chatbot to help users track their parcels, and my task was to design it. Before starting, I interviewed 8 users, and I found that most of them didn't want to chat, they just wanted to see their parcel status at a glance. So I proposed a simple tracking card on the home screen instead. The team agreed to test it. As a result, parcel status enquiries to customer support dropped by 25 percent in the first month, and we saved several weeks of chatbot development.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to balance user needs with business goals or technical limits.":
    "During my internship, users wanted a one tap checkout, while the business needed customers to see extra item offers before paying. Engineering also said a full redesign wasn't possible in the timeline. My task was to find a balance. I designed a small offer card that users could skip, placed above the payment button, using existing components so engineering could build it in three days. I tested it with 6 users to make sure it didn't feel pushy. As a result, checkout time stayed almost the same, and extra item sales grew by 9 percent.",
  "Describe how you presented a design to stakeholders and handled their questions.":
    "During my internship, I presented a redesign of our onboarding screens to the product manager, the marketing lead and two engineers. My goal was to get approval in one meeting. I started with the problem, showing that 45 percent of new users left during onboarding, then walked them through the new flow using a clickable prototype. When marketing asked why I removed a promotion screen, I showed test results where users skipped it 80 percent of the time. As a result, the design was approved with one small change, and onboarding completion later improved by 20 percent.",
  "Describe a time a developer or product manager pushed back on your design. How did you handle it?":
    "During my internship, a developer pushed back on my design for an animated progress bar, saying it would take a week to build. My task was to find a solution without losing the user benefit. So I asked him to explain what made it difficult, and I learned the animation was the expensive part. I suggested a simpler static version that still showed progress clearly. I also tested both with 5 users, and they performed equally well. As a result, the feature was built in two days, and form completion improved by 15 percent.",
  "Tell me about a design decision that did not work well once it was live. What did you learn?":
    "During my internship, I designed a new navigation that hid less used features under a more menu to make the home screen cleaner. After launch, usage of the saved items feature dropped by 30 percent, because users couldn't find it. My task was to fix it quickly. I looked at the analytics, spoke to 5 users, and confirmed the problem. I then moved saved items back to the main bar and tested it before release. As a result, usage recovered fully within two weeks. I learned to check the analytics for every feature before changing navigation.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to design under a very tight deadline. What did you compromise on?":
    "During my internship, I had only three days to design a registration page for an event campaign. My task was to deliver something usable on time. I decided to compromise on visual polish and custom illustrations, and I used existing design system components instead. I didn't compromise on clarity, so I still ran a quick test with 4 colleagues to make sure the form was easy to complete. As a result, the page launched on time, around 1,500 people registered, and we improved the visuals in the following sprint.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Behavioural: Customer Support                                          */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "Tell me about a time you helped a customer or a member of the public with a problem.":
    "During my part time job at a phone shop, an elderly customer came in because she couldn't receive messages from her family. My task was to solve it without overwhelming her. So I asked a few simple questions, checked her phone, and found her storage was completely full. I explained the issue in simple words, helped her back up her photos, and cleared about 5 gigabytes of space. Then I showed her how to check it herself. As a result, her messages worked again within 20 minutes, and she came back the next week to thank us.",
  "Describe a time you had to stay patient with someone who was frustrated.":
    "During my part time job at a café, a customer got frustrated because his order was taking much longer than expected during the lunch rush. My task was to calm the situation without making other customers wait longer. So I stayed calm, apologised, and explained honestly that we were short staffed. I checked his order status and gave him a clear time of five more minutes. I also offered him a free drink while he waited. As a result, he calmed down, received his food in four minutes, and later left a five star review.",
  "Tell me about a time you went the extra mile for someone.":
    "During my internship at a customer service centre, a customer urgently needed a replacement document for a visa appointment the next morning. Normally, it would take three working days. My task was to see what was possible. I explained the situation to my supervisor, found the right person in the documents team, and asked if they could prioritise it. I then stayed back for one extra hour to send it. As a result, the customer received the document by email within 5 hours, and she sent a thank you note to our manager.",
  "Tell me about a time you dealt with an angry customer. What did you say, and how did it end?":
    "During my internship in a customer support team, a customer called very angry because his parcel was a week late and nobody had updated him. My task was to calm him down and solve it. So I let him finish speaking, then said, I'm sorry, you should have been updated, and I'll find out exactly where your parcel is now. I checked the system, contacted the courier, and called him back within 30 minutes with a delivery date. As a result, he received his parcel two days later and rated our support five out of five.",
  "Describe a time you could not give a customer what they wanted. How did you handle it?":
    "During my internship, a customer wanted a full refund for an item she had used for two months, which was outside our 14 day refund policy. My task was to say no without losing her trust. So I explained the policy clearly and kindly, and then I focused on what I could offer. I checked her warranty and found the item could be repaired for free. I arranged the repair and gave her a tracking number. As a result, she accepted the solution, her item was repaired in five days, and she stayed a customer.",
  "Describe a time you worked with another team to solve a customer's problem.":
    "During my internship, several customers reported that their discount codes were not working at checkout. My task was to get it fixed quickly. So I collected five examples with screenshots and order details, then contacted the technical team with a clear summary. Together we found that codes failed when customers had items from two different sellers. While they worked on a fix, I agreed with my supervisor to apply discounts manually for affected customers. As a result, the bug was fixed in two days, and we handled 60 affected orders without a single escalation.",
  "Tell me about a time you noticed a recurring customer complaint and helped fix the root cause.":
    "During my internship in customer support, I noticed that many tickets every week were about customers not receiving their account verification email. My task was to find out why. So I tagged these tickets for two weeks and found about 80 of them, mostly from customers using one type of email provider. I shared the data with the technical team, who found our emails were being marked as spam, and they fixed the email settings. As a result, verification tickets dropped by 70 percent the following month, which freed up time for more complex cases.",
  "Describe a time you had many urgent tickets or calls at once. How did you prioritise?":
    "During a system outage in my internship, our support queue jumped from about 20 tickets to over 150 in two hours. My task was to help the team prioritise. I suggested we first separate tickets by impact, so customers who had paid but not received their order went to the top. I used a saved reply for general outage questions so we could respond to those quickly. Then I handled payment issues one by one. As a result, we responded to all urgent payment cases within one hour and cleared the queue by the end of the day.",
  "Tell me about a time you made a mistake with a customer. How did you put it right?":
    "During my internship, I gave a customer the wrong delivery date because I read the wrong order in the system. She arranged to be home that day, but the parcel didn't arrive. My task was to fix my mistake properly. I called her, admitted the error, and apologised sincerely. I then confirmed the correct date with the courier and arranged a free delivery slot that suited her. Afterwards, I started double checking the order number before replying. As a result, she received her parcel two days later, and I've had zero similar errors since.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Technical: general                                                     */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "What is the most important tool or method in your workflow, and why do you prefer it?":
    "The most important tool in my workflow is SQL, because almost every question I work on starts with getting the right data. I prefer it because it's precise, repeatable, and easy for others to review. For example, during my internship, the team was copying data from three systems into Excel every week. I wrote a set of SQL queries that pulled and combined the data automatically, and saved them so anyone could rerun them. As a result, the weekly preparation time dropped from four hours to about 20 minutes, and copy and paste errors disappeared.",
  "Explain a complex concept in your field as if you were explaining it to a non-technical colleague.":
    "I'd explain how a database index works. Imagine a 500 page textbook. If you want to find every page about photosynthesis, you could read the whole book, or you could check the index at the back, which tells you the exact pages. A database index works the same way. It helps the system jump straight to the right records instead of checking every row. During my internship, I explained this to an operations manager when our report was slow. After we added an index, the report went from 40 seconds to 2 seconds.",
  "Which software or tools are you most comfortable with, and how did you learn them?":
    "I'm most comfortable with SQL, Excel, Power BI and Python. I learned SQL and Python in my degree, but I really got good at them during my internship by using them on real data every day. I learned Power BI on my own through free online courses and by rebuilding existing reports. For example, I rebuilt a 12 tab Excel report into a single Power BI dashboard within three weeks. As a result, the managers could filter by region themselves, and report requests to our team dropped by about half.",
  "How do you structure your problem-solving process when facing an ambiguous problem with missing information?":
    "When a problem is unclear, I start by writing down what I know, what I don't know, and what decision the answer will support. Then I list a few possible explanations and test the cheapest ones first. During my internship, I was told that customer orders were down but nobody knew why. I clarified the time period, checked the data quality, and then broke orders down by region, channel and product. The drop came from one region where a delivery partner had stopped service. As a result, we found the cause in two days and recovered most of the lost orders within three weeks.",
  "Describe how you test, validate and check your work before handing it over to stakeholders.":
    "I check my work in three layers. First, I check the inputs, making sure the data is complete and matches the source system totals. Second, I check the logic, by testing my formulas or queries on a small sample where I already know the answer. Third, I ask whether the result makes sense, and if a number looks surprising, I trace it back. During my internship, this process caught a duplicated join that would have inflated revenue by 18 percent. Since I started using it, none of my 25 weekly reports had to be corrected after sending.",
  "Tell me about a time you had to troubleshoot a problem you had never seen before. What steps did you take?":
    "During my internship, our dashboard suddenly stopped refreshing, and I'd never seen the error before. My task was to fix it before the morning meeting. First, I read the error message carefully and searched the official documentation. Next, I checked what had changed recently and found that a database password had been updated the day before. I then updated the stored connection details with the data team and tested the refresh. As a result, the dashboard was working again within 90 minutes, and I wrote a short guide so the team could fix it themselves next time.",
  "How do you organise your work and documentation so someone else could pick it up if you were away?":
    "I organise my work so anyone could pick it up without calling me. Every project has one folder with a short readme explaining the purpose, the data sources, and how to run it. I keep my queries and scripts in version control with clear commit messages, and I write a short handover note at the end of each week. During my internship, I went on leave for a week, and a colleague had to run my monthly report. As a result, she completed it in under two hours using my notes, without contacting me once, and the report went out on time.",
  "Describe a project you are proud of from a planning and execution point of view. What trade-offs did you make?":
    "I'm proud of my final year project, a campus shuttle tracking app built with three teammates over 14 weeks. I planned it in two week cycles, with a working version at the end of each cycle. The main trade off was choosing a simple polling approach over real time sockets. It was less elegant, but we could build it in days instead of weeks. We also dropped a route planning feature to protect testing time. As a result, we delivered on time, tested with 60 students, and achieved an average rating of 4.5 out of 5.",
  "If you were given a task in an area you know very little about, with a one-week deadline, how would you approach it?":
    "First, I'd spend the first day understanding the goal: what decision or output is needed, and what a good result is. Then I'd learn just enough to start, using official documentation and asking someone experienced for 30 minutes of guidance. I'd build a rough first version by day three and share it early for feedback, leaving the last two days for fixes. During my internship, I had to prepare a basic inventory forecast with no forecasting background. I followed this approach, and as a result, I delivered a working forecast in five days that was within 10 percent of actual stock needs.",
  "How would you measure whether a piece of work you delivered actually achieved its goal?":
    "I measure success by agreeing on the goal and the metric before I start, and recording a baseline. Then I measure the same metric after delivery, and I also ask the users for feedback. During my internship, I built a dashboard to reduce the time managers spent preparing weekly reports. Before launch, I recorded that it took them about three hours. One month after launch, I checked again, and as a result I could show the time had dropped to about 40 minutes. I also tracked that 9 out of 10 managers opened it every week, which confirmed it was being used, not just delivered.",
  "Tell me about a technical decision you later realised was wrong. How did you fix it?":
    "In my final year project, I decided to store user session data in the browser's local storage to keep things simple. Later, during a security review with our supervisor, I realised this exposed tokens to possible script attacks. My task was to fix it without delaying our demo. I moved the session to secure, HTTP only cookies and added basic input sanitisation. I also wrote a short note explaining the change to my team. As a result, the fix took two days, our security checklist score improved from 6 to 9 out of 10, and we still delivered on time.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Technical: Associate Product Analyst                                   */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "What is the difference between daily active users and retention, and why would you track both?":
    "Daily active users counts how many unique users open the product on a given day, so it tells you about current usage. Retention measures how many users come back after a period, such as day 7 or day 30, so it tells you whether the product keeps its value over time. You need both, because daily active users can grow from a big campaign even if most new users never return. In my final year project, our daily active users doubled after a launch event, but day 7 retention was only 15 percent, which showed us we needed to improve onboarding.",
  "How would you build a simple funnel to see where users drop off in a food delivery app's checkout?":
    "I'd define the key steps first: open the restaurant page, add to cart, view cart, enter address, choose payment, and order confirmed. Then I'd count the unique users reaching each step in a given period, using event data, and calculate the conversion rate between each step. The biggest drop shows where to investigate. During my internship, I built a similar funnel in SQL for an online store and found that 35 percent of users left at the payment step. After the team added more payment options, that step's conversion improved by 12 percent.",
  "How would you explain conversion rate to a new teammate, and how is it calculated?":
    "I'd say conversion rate is the percentage of people who complete the action we care about, out of everyone who had the chance to. It's calculated as conversions divided by total visitors or users, times 100. For example, if 2,000 people visit a product page and 60 of them buy, the conversion rate is 3 percent. I'd also stress being clear about what counts as the action and who is in the starting group. During my internship, two teams reported different conversion rates simply because one counted sessions and the other counted users, and agreeing on one definition fixed the confusion.",
  "Explain the difference between leading and lagging indicators using a realistic customer funnel example.":
    "A lagging indicator shows the final outcome after it happens, such as monthly revenue or churn. A leading indicator moves earlier and helps predict that outcome, so you can act in time. Take an online grocery app. A lagging indicator is the number of customers who cancel their subscription each month. A leading indicator could be customers who haven't placed an order in 14 days, or who contacted support twice in a week. During my internship, we tracked inactive users weekly, and reaching out to them early reduced monthly churn by about 5 percent.",
  "If qualitative user feedback conflicts with analytics data, how do you decide what is true?":
    "I don't treat it as one being right and the other wrong. Analytics tell me what is happening and how often, while feedback tells me why. When they conflict, I check who each source represents. Feedback often comes from a small, vocal group, while analytics covers everyone. During my internship, users complained loudly that a new search was worse, but analytics showed search usage was stable. When I segmented the data, I found that power users, about 8 percent of users, really were searching less. We improved the advanced filters for that group, and their usage recovered within a month.",
  "Which core metrics, such as acquisition cost, lifetime value, retention, churn or NPS, would you monitor for a subscription app, and why?":
    "For a subscription app, I'd monitor all five. Acquisition cost tells me how much we spend to get each new subscriber. Lifetime value tells me how much revenue a subscriber brings over time, and it should be at least three times the acquisition cost. Retention and churn show whether people keep paying, and they're the clearest sign of product value. NPS gives a quick view of satisfaction and word of mouth. In a class case study, we found that churn of 9 percent a month turned a profitable acquisition plan into a losing one, so retention would be my first priority.",
  "How would you design an A/B test for a new onboarding flow? Explain the hypothesis, sample size and success metrics.":
    "First, I'd write a clear hypothesis, for example: reducing onboarding from five screens to three will increase the share of new users who complete their first key action within 24 hours. The primary metric would be that activation rate, with guardrail metrics such as day 7 retention. I'd randomly split new users into control and variant. For sample size, if the current activation rate is 40 percent and we want to detect a 3 point lift, a sample size calculator gives roughly 4,000 users per group. I'd run it for at least two full weeks to cover weekday patterns.",
  "How would you investigate a sudden drop in a mobile app's weekly active users?":
    "First, I'd confirm the drop is real and not a tracking issue, by checking whether event logging or the analytics setup changed. Next, I'd segment the drop by platform, app version, country, new versus returning users, and acquisition channel, to see where it's concentrated. Then I'd check for internal changes, such as a recent release, and external causes, such as a public holiday. During my internship, a similar 15 percent drop turned out to come only from Android users on one app version, after an update broke login. The fix restored the numbers within a week.",
  "How would you estimate the opportunity of adding a buy now, pay later option to an e-commerce checkout?":
    "I'd start with a simple top down estimate. First, I'd find how many checkouts are abandoned and how many of those mention price or payment issues. Then I'd estimate what share of those users might complete the purchase if they could split the payment, using industry benchmarks and a quick survey. For example, with 100,000 monthly checkouts, 20 percent abandoned, and buy now, pay later recovering 10 percent of those, that's 2,000 extra orders a month. I'd then subtract provider fees and risk, and validate the estimate with a small pilot before a full rollout.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Technical: Software Engineer                                           */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "What happens, step by step, when you type a URL into a browser and press Enter?":
    "First, the browser checks its cache and then asks DNS to turn the domain name into an IP address. Next, it opens a TCP connection to the server, and for HTTPS, it does a TLS handshake to set up encryption. Then it sends an HTTP request, and the server, often through a load balancer, returns a response with HTML. The browser parses the HTML, downloads the CSS, JavaScript and images, builds the page and renders it. During my internship, understanding this helped me find that a slow page was waiting on 30 unnecessary script files, and bundling them cut load time by half.",
  "What is the difference between git merge and git rebase, and when would you use each?":
    "Git merge combines two branches and creates a merge commit, keeping the full history exactly as it happened. Git rebase moves your commits on top of another branch, which gives a clean, straight history but rewrites those commits. I use rebase on my own feature branch to stay up to date with main before opening a pull request, so reviewers see a clean history. I use merge for shared branches, because rewriting history that others use causes problems. During my internship, our team adopted this rule, and merge conflicts during reviews dropped from several a week to about one.",
  "What is the difference between a stack and a queue? Give a real use for each.":
    "A stack is last in, first out, so the most recently added item is removed first. Think of a pile of plates. A queue is first in, first out, so items are processed in the order they arrive, the same as people waiting in line at a bank counter. A real use for a stack is the undo feature in a text editor, where the last action is reversed first. A real use for a queue is processing tasks such as sending emails in order. In my final year project, I used a queue to process 500 booking notifications without losing any during busy periods.",
  "Explain how RESTful API design differs from GraphQL or RPC, and when you would choose each.":
    "REST organises an API around resources, using standard HTTP methods on URLs, such as GET on orders. It's simple, cacheable and widely understood. GraphQL uses a single endpoint where the client asks for exactly the fields it needs, which helps when screens need data from many sources. RPC, such as gRPC, calls functions directly and is fast and strongly typed, so it suits internal calls between services. I'd choose REST for most public APIs, GraphQL for complex front ends, and RPC for high performance internal systems. In my final year project, one combined query cut a screen's API calls from six to one.",
  "Describe how you handle asynchronous state, concurrency and caching in a modern web app.":
    "I separate server state from local UI state. For server data, I use a data fetching library that handles loading, errors, caching and refetching, instead of managing it all manually. For concurrency, I make sure only the latest request updates the screen by cancelling outdated requests, and I disable buttons during submission to prevent double actions. For caching, I set sensible stale times and invalidate the cache after a change. During my internship, adding request caching to our orders page reduced API calls by about 60 percent and made navigation feel instant.",
  "How do you decide what to cover with unit tests and what to cover with integration tests?":
    "I use unit tests for pure logic with many cases, such as price calculations, validation rules and date handling, because they're fast and pinpoint errors. I use integration tests for the important paths where components meet, such as an API endpoint that writes to the database or a checkout flow, because most real bugs happen at those boundaries. I prioritise by risk: what would hurt users or money most if it broke. During my internship, I added integration tests for our five main API endpoints, and they caught two bugs before release that our unit tests had missed.",
  "How do you design database indexes for high-throughput queries, and what are the trade-offs?":
    "I start from the actual queries, looking at the WHERE, JOIN and ORDER BY columns of the most frequent and slowest ones, and I use the query plan to confirm. For queries filtering on several columns, I create composite indexes with the most selective equality columns first. The trade offs are that each index uses storage and slows down inserts and updates, so I avoid indexing everything. During my internship, a report query filtered by customer and date. Adding one composite index on those two columns cut it from 12 seconds to under 200 milliseconds.",
  "Walk me through how you would secure a web application against common OWASP Top 10 risks such as SQL injection, XSS, CSRF and broken authentication.":
    "For SQL injection, I always use parameterised queries or an ORM and never build queries by joining strings. For XSS, I rely on the framework's automatic escaping, avoid inserting raw HTML, and add a content security policy. For CSRF, I use anti CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies. For broken authentication, I hash passwords with a strong algorithm, add rate limiting, use multi factor authentication where possible, and keep sessions in secure, HTTP only cookies. In my final year project, a security scan found 7 issues, and these steps brought it down to zero high risk findings.",
  "How would you design a system that sends payment reminders to a very large number of users without overloading your servers?":
    "I'd avoid sending everything at once. A scheduler would find users due for a reminder and push jobs into a message queue in batches. A pool of workers would then process the queue at a controlled rate, calling the email or SMS provider with retries and backoff for failures. I'd make each job idempotent, so a retry never sends a duplicate reminder, and spread the sending over a time window. I'd also monitor queue length and failure rates. In a class project, this approach let us send 100,000 test reminders in an hour with stable server load.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Technical: Data Analyst                                                */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "What is the difference between an INNER JOIN and a LEFT JOIN? Give an example of when you would use each.":
    "An INNER JOIN returns only rows that have a match in both tables. A LEFT JOIN returns every row from the left table, and fills in matching data from the right table, or nulls when there's no match. I'd use an INNER JOIN to list orders with their customer details, because every order should have a customer. I'd use a LEFT JOIN to find customers who have never ordered, by joining customers to orders and filtering where the order is null. During my internship, that LEFT JOIN query found 1,200 inactive customers for a marketing campaign.",
  "Which chart would you use to show monthly sales over a year, and which would you avoid? Why?":
    "For monthly sales over a year, I'd use a line chart, or a column chart if there are only a few series, because both show the trend over time clearly and the eye follows the pattern from month to month. I'd avoid a pie chart, because pies show parts of a whole, not change over time, and comparing twelve slices is very hard. I'd also avoid 3D charts that distort values. During my internship, I replaced a pie chart in a monthly report with a simple line chart, and managers spotted a three month decline they had missed before.",
  "How would you remove duplicate rows from a dataset, in Excel or in SQL?":
    "In Excel, I'd first decide which columns define a duplicate, make a backup copy, and then use the Remove Duplicates feature on those columns. In SQL, I'd use SELECT DISTINCT for simple cases, or for more control, ROW_NUMBER partitioned by the key columns and ordered by the latest update, keeping only row number one. That way I keep the most recent record. I always count rows before and after to check the result. During my internship, this approach removed about 3,000 duplicate customer records out of 40,000, which fixed inflated customer counts in our reports.",
  "How do you ensure data accuracy, validate assumptions and avoid misleading conclusions?":
    "First, I check that the data is complete by comparing row counts and totals against the source system. Next, I write down my assumptions, such as how refunds are handled, and confirm them with the data owner. Then I test my logic on a small sample I can verify by hand. Before concluding, I look for alternative explanations and check whether the pattern holds across segments and time periods. During my internship, this process showed that a 20 percent sales jump was caused by a duplicated data load, which stopped a wrong conclusion from reaching management.",
  "Walk me through a SQL query you wrote using window functions such as ROW_NUMBER, LAG or LEAD with PARTITION BY.":
    "During my internship, I needed to find each customer's time between orders. I used LAG with PARTITION BY customer ID, ordered by order date, to get the previous order date on each row, and then calculated the gap in days. I also used ROW_NUMBER partitioned by customer and ordered by date to label each customer's first order, so I could separate new and repeat purchases. The query showed that customers who ordered again within 30 days were three times more likely to become regular buyers, and the team used this to time their follow up promotions.",
  "Explain the difference between OLTP and OLAP systems, including star and snowflake schemas.":
    "OLTP systems handle day to day transactions, such as creating orders or updating stock, with many small, fast reads and writes, so they're highly normalised. OLAP systems are built for analysis across large volumes of historical data, such as sales by region over three years. They often use a star schema, with a central fact table, such as sales, linked to dimension tables, such as date, product and store. A snowflake schema further normalises those dimensions into sub tables, saving space but adding joins. In my final year project, a star schema made our dashboard queries about five times faster.",
  "How do you detect anomalies or outliers in time-series data without throwing away valuable signals?":
    "First, I account for trend and seasonality, because a spike every Friday or during festive seasons isn't an anomaly. I compare each value with what's expected, using a rolling average or a seasonal baseline, and flag values beyond a threshold, such as three standard deviations. Instead of deleting outliers, I investigate and label them, because some are real signals, such as a successful promotion or a system outage. During my internship, this approach flagged 6 unusual days in a year of sales data, and two of them revealed a pricing error worth fixing.",
  "Sales went up after a marketing campaign. How would you check whether the campaign actually caused it?":
    "I'd first compare sales with a baseline, such as the same period last year and the weeks before the campaign, to remove seasonality. Better still, I'd look for a comparison group, such as regions or customers who didn't see the campaign, and compare their change with the campaign group. I'd also check other factors, such as price changes, public holidays or competitor events. If possible, I'd recommend a holdout group for future campaigns. During my internship, this approach showed that only about 5 percent of a 15 percent sales increase came from the campaign itself.",
  "How would you design a dashboard for a retail chain's regional managers? What goes on the first screen, and why?":
    "I'd start by asking regional managers what decisions they make each week, because the dashboard should support those decisions. On the first screen, I'd show a few key metrics for their region: sales against target, growth versus last year, and the top and bottom performing stores, with clear red and green indicators. Below that, a trend line and a store ranking. Details such as product breakdowns would go on separate pages with filters. During my internship, a similar layout reduced the time managers spent finding issues from about an hour to ten minutes each week.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Technical: Product Manager                                             */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "How would you write a good user story for a feature that lets users save items to a wishlist?":
    "I'd write it from the user's point of view with a clear reason: as a shopper, I want to save items to a wishlist so that I can come back and buy them later without searching again. Then I'd add acceptance criteria. The user can save an item with one tap, the wishlist is visible from the profile, saved items stay after logging out, and the user is notified when an item's price drops. During my internship, adding clear acceptance criteria to our stories cut back and forth questions from developers by about half.",
  "What is the difference between an MVP and a full product launch?":
    "An MVP is the smallest version of a product that lets you test your main assumption with real users, so the goal is learning, not perfection. A full launch is when the product is complete, reliable and ready for a wide audience, with marketing, support and scaling in place. For example, in my final year project, our MVP for a tutoring app was just a simple form and a manual matching process. It helped us learn that 70 percent of students wanted evening sessions, which shaped our full version before we invested in building it properly.",
  "What would you include in a simple product requirements document for a new feature?":
    "I'd keep it short and clear. First, the problem and why it matters, with supporting data. Second, the goals and success metrics. Third, the target users and key user stories. Fourth, the scope, listing what's included and, just as important, what's not. Then the requirements and acceptance criteria, any designs, dependencies and risks, and a rough timeline. During my internship, I wrote a two page requirements document for a saved address feature using this structure, and the team estimated and started work after one meeting instead of the usual three.",
  "How would you prioritise a backlog of twenty feature requests? Which framework would you use, and why?":
    "I'd first group similar requests and clarify the problem behind each one, because twenty requests often become twelve real problems. Then I'd use RICE, scoring each by reach, impact, confidence and effort, because it forces us to back opinions with numbers and makes trade offs visible to stakeholders. I'd also check each item against the current product goal. During my internship, I scored 18 requests with RICE, and the top three, which together affected about 40 percent of users, were delivered first. The rest went into a transparent roadmap that stakeholders could review.",
  "Which metrics would you track for an e-wallet app in Malaysia, and which one would be your north star metric?":
    "For an e-wallet app in Malaysia, I'd track new user activation, monthly transacting users, transactions per user, total payment value, retention by cohort, and failed transaction rate. I'd also watch merchant adoption, because more merchants make the wallet more useful. My north star metric would be monthly transacting users, because it shows people get real value from the wallet, rather than just downloading it for a promotion. In a class project, we found that users who made three transactions in their first week were twice as likely to stay active after three months.",
  "How would you define success for a new referral programme in a ride-hailing app?":
    "I'd define success by the quality of new users, not just the number. My main metric would be referred users who complete their first ride within 14 days. I'd also track the referral share rate, the cost per new rider compared with other channels, and the 30 day retention of referred users versus normal users. Guardrails would include fraud, such as fake accounts claiming rewards. Success might mean referred riders costing 30 percent less than paid ads with similar retention. In a class case study, we found referred users had retention 10 percent higher than users from paid ads.",
  "Checkout conversion dropped after a redesign. Walk me through how you would diagnose it and decide whether to roll back.":
    "First, I'd confirm the drop is real, by checking for tracking changes and comparing the same days of the week. Next, I'd segment it by device, app version, payment method and new versus returning users, and look at the funnel step by step to see where users leave. I'd also check error logs and session recordings. If the drop is large and clearly tied to the redesign, I'd roll back or turn off the feature flag while we investigate. During my internship, a similar drop came from a hidden promo code field, and fixing it restored conversion within a week.",
  "How would you decide whether to build a feature in-house, buy a third-party tool or partner with another company?":
    "I'd look at four questions. First, is this core to our product and a source of competitive advantage? If yes, building in house makes sense. Second, how fast do we need it, and what's the total cost over two or three years, including maintenance? Third, does a reliable third party tool already solve it well? Fourth, would a partner bring something we can't easily build, such as customers or licences? For example, in a class case study on payment processing, buying a ready payment service saved an estimated six months and reduced compliance risk compared with building our own.",
  "Engineering tells you the release is two weeks behind. How would you decide what to cut?":
    "First, I'd understand the cause of the delay and confirm the new estimate with the engineers. Then I'd go back to the goal of the release and list every remaining item, ranking them by user impact and risk. I'd protect anything required for the core user value, security or legal reasons, and cut or defer the items that are only nice to have. I'd also check whether a smaller release or a feature flag could ship the core on time. During my internship, this approach let us cut two minor features and still launch on the original date.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Technical: UX Designer                                                 */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "What is the difference between UX design and UI design?":
    "UX design is about the whole experience of using a product: understanding user needs, structuring the flow, and making sure people can complete their tasks easily. UI design focuses on the visual and interactive layer: colours, typography, buttons, spacing and how elements respond. A simple way to put it is that UX decides a checkout needs only three steps, while UI decides how those steps look and feel. In my final year project, I worked on both, and improving the flow first increased task success by 30 percent before we even polished the visuals.",
  "What is a wireframe, and when would you use a low-fidelity prototype instead of a high-fidelity one?":
    "A wireframe is a simple layout of a screen that shows structure and content placement without visual detail, similar to a blueprint for a house. I use low fidelity prototypes early, when I'm exploring ideas and want quick feedback on the flow, because they're fast to change and people feel comfortable criticising them. I switch to high fidelity prototypes when the flow is settled and I need to test details such as wording, visual hierarchy or interactions. In my final year project, testing paper sketches with 5 users helped me fix two major flow problems in one afternoon.",
  "What is a user persona, and how would you build one from real research?":
    "A user persona is a realistic profile representing a group of users with similar goals, behaviours and frustrations. It helps the team keep real people in mind when making decisions. To build one from research, I'd interview users, then cluster the notes by patterns in goals and behaviours, not by age or job title alone. Each persona would include goals, pain points, context and a few real quotes. In my final year project, I interviewed 12 students and found two distinct groups, early planners and last minute users, which led us to design two different booking shortcuts.",
  "How would you plan a usability test for a new mobile banking feature? Who would you recruit, and what would you measure?":
    "First, I'd define the goal, for example, can users set up a recurring transfer without help? I'd recruit about 5 to 8 participants who match the real users, including a mix of ages, digital confidence, and both Bahasa Melayu and English speakers. I'd write realistic tasks and ask them to think aloud. I'd measure task success rate, time on task, errors and a short satisfaction score after each task. In a similar test during my internship, 3 out of 6 users couldn't find the transfer schedule option, which led to a quick redesign.",
  "How do you use components and auto layout in Figma to keep a design system consistent?":
    "I build a base library of components, such as buttons, inputs and cards, with variants for states and sizes, so every screen uses the same source. When I update a main component, the change flows to every instance. Auto layout lets components resize with their content, so a button grows with longer text, and lists keep consistent spacing. I also use shared colour and text styles. During my internship, I rebuilt our button and form components with variants and auto layout, which reduced design inconsistencies flagged in reviews from about 15 per sprint to 3.",
  "How do you turn notes from user interviews into clear design insights?":
    "After each interview, I write down key observations while they're fresh, separating what users said from what they did. Then I put every observation on a digital sticky note and group them by theme in an affinity map. From each cluster, I write an insight that explains the pattern and why it matters, for example: users don't trust the balance shown because it updates late. Each insight links back to evidence and a design opportunity. In my final year project, this process turned 10 interviews and over 200 notes into five clear insights in one afternoon.",
  "How do you make a design accessible for users with low vision or users who rely on screen readers?":
    "For users with low vision, I make sure text and buttons meet colour contrast guidelines, support larger font sizes without breaking the layout, and never rely on colour alone to show meaning. For screen reader users, I work with developers to add clear labels to buttons and images, use proper headings, and keep a logical focus order. I test with a screen reader myself, and with real users where possible. In my final year project, an accessibility review found 14 issues, and after fixing them, a visually impaired tester could complete the main task without help.",
  "A form in a government services app has a high drop-off rate. How would you find out why and redesign it?":
    "First, I'd look at the analytics to find exactly which fields or steps users leave on, and check error rates for each field. Then I'd watch a few users complete the form, and interview them to understand why they get stuck, such as unclear wording, documents they don't have, or session timeouts. Based on that, I'd redesign it by splitting the form into short steps, using plain language in both Bahasa Melayu and English, saving progress automatically, and showing the required documents upfront. In a similar class project, these changes reduced the number of users quitting the form by 30 percent.",
  "How would you design an app for users who switch comfortably between Bahasa Melayu and English?":
    "I'd make language switching easy and always visible, for example on the first screen and in settings, and remember the user's choice. I'd design layouts with flexible space, because Bahasa Melayu text is often longer than English. I'd write content in both languages from the start, not just translate at the end, and test both versions with real users. I'd also support mixed language search, so typing in either language finds the same result. In my final year project, adding a clear language toggle and bilingual search increased task completion for Malay speakers by 20 percent.",

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Technical: Customer Support                                            */
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  "What does good customer service look like over live chat compared with over the phone?":
    "Over live chat, good service means fast first responses, clear and short messages, and using links or screenshots to guide the customer. It also means handling a few chats at once without making anyone feel ignored. Over the phone, tone of voice matters much more, so I listen actively, confirm details out loud, and avoid long silences by explaining what I'm doing. In both, empathy and accuracy come first. During my internship, I handled about 40 chats a day, and keeping my first reply under one minute helped me maintain a satisfaction score of 4.7 out of 5.",
  "How would you write a clear and friendly reply to a customer asking the same question for the third time?":
    "I'd stay friendly and avoid any hint of frustration, because the customer probably still doesn't understand, which means my earlier explanation wasn't clear enough. I'd acknowledge it warmly, then explain it differently, in simple steps, perhaps with a screenshot or a short example. For example: thanks for checking again, let me show you a simpler way, first open your profile, then tap orders. I'd end by asking if it worked. During my internship, rewriting replies into numbered steps cut repeat questions on the same issue by about 30 percent.",
  "How would you greet a customer and verify their identity before discussing their account?":
    "I'd greet the customer warmly with my name, for example: good morning, thank you for contacting us, my name is Ali, how can I help you today? Before discussing any account details, I'd follow the company's verification process, such as confirming their registered email and phone number or sending a one time code, without ever asking for their full password. I'd explain that it's to protect their account. During my internship, I followed this process on about 50 calls a day, and none of them led to a security complaint.",
  "Which helpdesk or CRM tools have you used, and how did you use them to track and follow up on cases?":
    "During my internship, I used a helpdesk system to manage customer tickets and a simple CRM to view each customer's order history. I used tags to group tickets by issue type, such as delivery or refunds, and set reminders to follow up on cases waiting for another team. I also used saved replies for common questions, but always personalised them. Every Friday, I checked my open tickets and updated any that were waiting. As a result, my average resolution time dropped from about two days to one day over three months.",
  "A customer says they were charged twice for one online order. Walk me through the steps you would take.":
    "First, I'd apologise and reassure the customer that we'll check it. Then I'd verify their identity and ask for the order number and the dates of the charges. Next, I'd check our payment system to see whether there are two successful charges, or one charge and a temporary hold, which often disappears within a few days. If it's a real duplicate, I'd raise a refund with the payments team and give the customer a clear timeline. During my internship, I handled around 20 such cases, and clear timelines meant very few customers needed to follow up again.",
  "How do you write case notes so a colleague can continue without asking the customer to repeat themselves?":
    "I write case notes so someone new can understand the case in under a minute. I include the customer's issue in one sentence, what I've checked, what I've done, what was promised to the customer and by when, and the next step. I write in clear, neutral language and avoid internal shorthand. For example: customer charged twice, second charge confirmed, refund requested on Monday, customer told three to five working days, follow up on Friday. During my internship, this format meant colleagues could continue my cases without asking customers to repeat themselves.",
  "How do you decide when to escalate a case to a supervisor or another team, and what information do you include?":
    "I escalate when the issue is outside my authority or knowledge, when the customer is at risk of financial loss, when it's taking longer than our service level target, or when the same problem is affecting many customers. Before escalating, I include the customer details, a short summary, what I've already tried, evidence such as screenshots, the urgency, and what the customer has been told. During my internship, I escalated a payment issue affecting 15 customers with all of this information, and the technical team fixed it within a day without asking me any follow up questions.",
  "Which metrics, such as first response time, resolution time or customer satisfaction, would you use to judge support quality, and how can each one mislead?":
    "I'd use first response time, resolution time and customer satisfaction together. First response time shows how quickly we acknowledge customers, but it can mislead if agents send quick, empty replies just to stop the clock. Resolution time shows efficiency, but agents might close tickets too early, causing repeat contacts. Customer satisfaction captures the customer's view, but often only very happy or very unhappy people respond. So I'd add a repeat contact rate as a balance. During my internship, the team with the fastest response times also had a 25 percent reopen rate, which showed the problem.",
  "A product issue is causing many complaints on social media. How would you respond publicly and internally?":
    "Publicly, I'd respond quickly with a short, honest message: acknowledge the issue, apologise, explain we're working on it, and say when we'll update next. I'd reply to individual posts with a link to a single update page, and move personal cases to private messages. Internally, I'd alert the technical team with examples, share approved replies with the support team, and track the number of complaints. During my internship, a similar outage generated about 200 posts in a day, and posting updates every two hours helped cut new complaints by half by the next morning.",
}

